1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:02,956
It was a morning during the Second World

2
00:00:02,976 --> 00:00:05,341
War, at a railroad station somewhere on

3
00:00:05,361 --> 00:00:07,386
the line between Louisiana and Arizona.

4
00:00:07,406 --> 00:00:09,771
Nine American soldiers in uniform had been

5
00:00:09,791 --> 00:00:12,156
waiting there since the day before for

6
00:00:12,176 --> 00:00:12,837
their train.

7
00:00:12,857 --> 00:00:15,904
They were Black, and in that town not one

8
00:00:15,924 --> 00:00:18,650
lunchroom would sell them a cup of coffee.

9
00:00:19,950 --> 00:00:22,449
The only place that would feed them was

10
00:00:22,469 --> 00:00:24,338
the lunchroom at the station itself.

11
00:00:24,358 --> 00:00:27,486
But they were not allowed to sit down in it.

12
00:00:27,506 --> 00:00:29,710
They had to eat in the kitchen.

13
00:00:31,050 --> 00:00:33,669
Then, at eleven thirty, about two dozen

14
00:00:33,689 --> 00:00:36,307
German prisoners of war came into the

15
00:00:36,327 --> 00:00:38,192
station with two American guards.

16
00:00:38,212 --> 00:00:40,831
The Germans sat down at the tables.

17
00:00:40,851 --> 00:00:43,093
Their meals were served to them.

18
00:00:43,113 --> 00:00:46,108
They talked, they smoked, and one of the

19
00:00:46,128 --> 00:00:47,616
nine Americans stood outside,

20
00:00:47,636 --> 00:00:48,436
looking on.

21
00:00:49,675 --> 00:00:51,391
His name was Rupert Trimmingham,

22
00:00:51,411 --> 00:00:53,822
a corporal in the Corps of Engineers.

23
00:00:53,842 --> 00:00:56,947
What he saw that morning, he wrote down in

24
00:00:56,967 --> 00:00:57,641
a letter.

25
00:00:57,661 --> 00:01:00,072
And when the Army's own magazine printed

26
00:01:00,092 --> 00:01:02,175
it, hundreds of soldiers wrote back.

27
00:01:03,400 --> 00:01:05,777
This is the documented story of the German

28
00:01:05,797 --> 00:01:07,574
prisoners who were served where American

29
00:01:07,594 --> 00:01:08,473
soldiers were not.

30
00:01:08,493 --> 00:01:10,870
It is told from the letters the soldiers

31
00:01:10,890 --> 00:01:12,967
wrote, from the newspapers of the time,

32
00:01:12,987 --> 00:01:15,664
and from the words of a famous singer who

33
00:01:15,684 --> 00:01:18,380
saw the same thing and walked off the stage.

34
00:01:20,125 --> 00:01:21,208
To understand that morning,

35
00:01:21,228 --> 00:01:23,414
you have to know what the country looked

36
00:01:23,434 --> 00:01:26,185
like in 1944.

37
00:01:26,205 --> 00:01:28,040
About nine hundred thousand African

38
00:01:28,060 --> 00:01:30,637
Americans served in uniform during the war.

39
00:01:30,657 --> 00:01:32,863
They served in a segregated Army,

40
00:01:32,883 --> 00:01:35,832
in their own units, under rules that kept

41
00:01:35,852 --> 00:01:38,429
them apart from white soldiers in camps,

42
00:01:38,449 --> 00:01:40,675
in mess halls and in theaters.

43
00:01:41,375 --> 00:01:44,116
Off the base, in much of the country,

44
00:01:44,136 --> 00:01:45,841
the law was harder still.

45
00:01:45,861 --> 00:01:48,947
In the South it was written into every bus

46
00:01:48,967 --> 00:01:50,673
station and every restaurant door.

47
00:01:50,693 --> 00:01:52,744
The signs said "White" and "Colored",

48
00:01:52,764 --> 00:01:55,504
and a man in an American uniform was

49
00:01:55,524 --> 00:01:57,250
still, first of all, colored.

50
00:01:57,950 --> 00:02:00,146
Trimmingham was not a young man.

51
00:02:00,166 --> 00:02:02,731
He was born in Port of Spain,

52
00:02:02,751 --> 00:02:05,570
Trinidad, in 1899.

53
00:02:05,590 --> 00:02:07,807
He had been a merchant seaman out of Wales

54
00:02:07,827 --> 00:02:09,050
after the First World War,

55
00:02:09,070 --> 00:02:12,950
and he came to New York in 1925.

56
00:02:12,970 --> 00:02:15,170
He joined the Army Corps of Engineers in

57
00:02:15,190 --> 00:02:17,535
1942, as an electrician.

58
00:02:17,555 --> 00:02:20,130
He was forty-four years old.

59
00:02:21,375 --> 00:02:23,532
He was stationed at Fort Huachuca,

60
00:02:23,552 --> 00:02:26,435
in the Arizona desert, near the Mexican border.

61
00:02:26,455 --> 00:02:29,701
At the height of the war it held the

62
00:02:29,721 --> 00:02:31,878
largest concentration of Black soldiers in

63
00:02:31,898 --> 00:02:32,604
the country.

64
00:02:32,624 --> 00:02:34,055
The ninety-third Infantry Division,

65
00:02:34,075 --> 00:02:36,595
the first all-Black division formed in the

66
00:02:36,615 --> 00:02:39,155
war, was brought back to life there.

67
00:02:40,375 --> 00:02:43,128
He and eight other soldiers were on their

68
00:02:43,148 --> 00:02:45,208
way from Camp Claiborne, in Louisiana,

69
00:02:45,228 --> 00:02:47,288
to the hospital at Fort Huachuca.

70
00:02:47,308 --> 00:02:50,408
Somewhere on the way, they had to lay over

71
00:02:50,428 --> 00:02:51,795
until the next day.

72
00:02:51,815 --> 00:02:53,895
The letter never names the town.

73
00:02:55,600 --> 00:02:58,005
At the same time, another army was

74
00:02:58,025 --> 00:02:59,390
crossing America by train.

75
00:02:59,410 --> 00:03:01,468
They were German prisoners of war.

76
00:03:01,488 --> 00:03:03,546
More than three hundred and seventy

77
00:03:03,566 --> 00:03:06,317
thousand Germans would end up in camps in

78
00:03:06,337 --> 00:03:09,088
the United States, and two thirds of them

79
00:03:09,108 --> 00:03:10,840
were held in the South.

80
00:03:12,150 --> 00:03:15,202
The United States treated them by the book.

81
00:03:15,222 --> 00:03:20,070
The book was the Geneva Convention of 1929.

82
00:03:20,090 --> 00:03:22,065
Its eleventh article said the food of

83
00:03:22,085 --> 00:03:24,060
prisoners of war "shall be equal in

84
00:03:24,080 --> 00:03:26,340
quantity and quality to that of troops at

85
00:03:26,360 --> 00:03:27,160
base camps."

86
00:03:28,225 --> 00:03:30,963
So the prisoners ate the same rations as

87
00:03:30,983 --> 00:03:31,653
American soldiers.

88
00:03:31,673 --> 00:03:34,066
And when they travelled, they ate where

89
00:03:34,086 --> 00:03:35,465
their guards took them.

90
00:03:36,700 --> 00:03:38,642
The Convention said nothing about which

91
00:03:38,662 --> 00:03:40,277
room they should eat in.

92
00:03:40,297 --> 00:03:43,219
That was decided by the people who ran the

93
00:03:43,239 --> 00:03:46,471
lunchroom, and in 1944, in that town,

94
00:03:46,491 --> 00:03:48,414
those people decided that white enemy

95
00:03:48,434 --> 00:03:50,357
soldiers could sit at the tables,

96
00:03:50,377 --> 00:03:52,320
and Black American soldiers could not.

97
00:03:54,000 --> 00:03:56,198
Trimmingham went back to Fort Huachuca,

98
00:03:56,218 --> 00:03:59,156
and he wrote to Yank, the Army Weekly.

99
00:03:59,176 --> 00:04:01,744
Yank was written by enlisted men for

100
00:04:01,764 --> 00:04:02,484
enlisted men.

101
00:04:02,504 --> 00:04:05,442
At its peak it printed two million six

102
00:04:05,462 --> 00:04:07,680
hundred thousand copies of every issue.

103
00:04:08,925 --> 00:04:10,927
He began with a question.

104
00:04:10,947 --> 00:04:13,758
What is the Negro soldier fighting for?

105
00:04:13,778 --> 00:04:16,205
On whose team are we playing?

106
00:04:17,600 --> 00:04:19,554
He told them about the lunchroom.

107
00:04:19,574 --> 00:04:21,857
He wrote that the Germans "entered the

108
00:04:21,877 --> 00:04:23,503
lunchroom, sat at the tables,

109
00:04:23,523 --> 00:04:25,148
had their meals served, talked,

110
00:04:25,168 --> 00:04:27,800
smoked, in fact had quite a swell time."

111
00:04:29,075 --> 00:04:31,711
Then he wrote the lines that would be

112
00:04:31,731 --> 00:04:34,035
quoted for the rest of the century.

113
00:04:34,055 --> 00:04:36,691
"Are these men sworn enemies of this country?

114
00:04:36,711 --> 00:04:38,351
Are we not American soldiers,

115
00:04:38,371 --> 00:04:41,671
sworn to fight for and die if need be for

116
00:04:41,691 --> 00:04:42,667
this our country?

117
00:04:42,687 --> 00:04:45,323
Then why are they treated better than we

118
00:04:45,343 --> 00:04:46,143
are?"

119
00:04:46,775 --> 00:04:48,407
He ended with a bet.

120
00:04:48,427 --> 00:04:50,389
Some of the boys, he wrote,

121
00:04:50,409 --> 00:04:53,363
are saying that you will not print this letter.

122
00:04:53,383 --> 00:04:55,035
I'm saying that you will.

123
00:04:56,350 --> 00:04:59,498
Yank printed it, in the issue of April the

124
00:04:59,518 --> 00:05:01,150
twenty-eighth, 1944.

125
00:05:02,250 --> 00:05:04,966
The answers came from all over the world.

126
00:05:04,986 --> 00:05:07,702
In the June ninth issue, Yank printed a

127
00:05:07,722 --> 00:05:10,780
letter from a corporal at an air field in

128
00:05:10,800 --> 00:05:11,806
California, Henry Wootton.

129
00:05:11,826 --> 00:05:14,542
He wrote that he was a Southern rebel.

130
00:05:14,562 --> 00:05:16,936
"If this incident is democracy," he wrote,

131
00:05:16,956 --> 00:05:19,350
"I don't want any part of it!"

132
00:05:20,550 --> 00:05:22,203
A staff sergeant in Bermuda,

133
00:05:22,223 --> 00:05:24,210
Arthur Kaplan, thanked the editors for

134
00:05:24,230 --> 00:05:26,217
having the courage to print it.

135
00:05:26,237 --> 00:05:28,559
He asked whether anyone had thought that

136
00:05:28,579 --> 00:05:30,566
those German prisoners must still be

137
00:05:30,586 --> 00:05:31,590
laughing at us.

138
00:05:32,800 --> 00:05:34,695
A private at a port of embarkation,

139
00:05:34,715 --> 00:05:36,884
Gustave Santiago, wrote that he was not a

140
00:05:36,904 --> 00:05:38,525
Negro, but he had been around,

141
00:05:38,545 --> 00:05:40,460
and he knew what the score was.

142
00:05:41,725 --> 00:05:44,603
And from the jungle of Burma came a letter

143
00:05:44,623 --> 00:05:46,212
signed by four white soldiers:

144
00:05:46,232 --> 00:05:48,788
an Italian, a Frenchman, a Swede and an

145
00:05:48,808 --> 00:05:50,398
Irishman, as they described themselves.

146
00:05:50,418 --> 00:05:52,652
They wrote that Black units worked beside

147
00:05:52,672 --> 00:05:54,905
them and did more than their part.

148
00:05:54,925 --> 00:05:57,481
"We are proud of the colored men here,"

149
00:05:57,501 --> 00:05:58,301
they wrote.

150
00:05:59,400 --> 00:06:01,842
They had read the letter, and they wrote:

151
00:06:01,862 --> 00:06:03,997
Corporal Trimmingham asked what is a Negro

152
00:06:04,017 --> 00:06:04,612
fighting for?

153
00:06:04,632 --> 00:06:06,459
"If this sort of thing continues,

154
00:06:06,479 --> 00:06:08,613
we the white soldiers will begin to

155
00:06:08,633 --> 00:06:08,921
wonder:

156
00:06:08,941 --> 00:06:10,480
What are we fighting for?"

157
00:06:11,675 --> 00:06:13,920
Yank printed that letter on the

158
00:06:13,940 --> 00:06:16,186
twenty-eighth of July, under the heading

159
00:06:16,206 --> 00:06:18,074
Democracy, with a question mark.

160
00:06:18,094 --> 00:06:21,850
Next to it was a second letter from Fort Huachuca.

161
00:06:21,870 --> 00:06:24,115
Trimmingham thanked them for printing his

162
00:06:24,135 --> 00:06:24,870
first one.

163
00:06:24,890 --> 00:06:27,891
Then he told them what had happened since.

164
00:06:27,911 --> 00:06:30,156
"To date I've received 287 letters,

165
00:06:30,176 --> 00:06:32,422
and, strange as it may seem,

166
00:06:32,442 --> 00:06:35,820
183 are from white men and women in the

167
00:06:35,840 --> 00:06:36,640
armed service."

168
00:06:37,925 --> 00:06:41,126
Most of them, he wrote, were from the Deep South.

169
00:06:41,146 --> 00:06:43,058
They were proud of the South,

170
00:06:43,078 --> 00:06:43,702
and ashamed.

171
00:06:43,722 --> 00:06:45,312
He called it new hope.

172
00:06:45,332 --> 00:06:47,567
All that the Negro is asking for,

173
00:06:47,587 --> 00:06:50,485
he wrote, is to be given half a chance.

174
00:06:51,850 --> 00:06:53,605
The editors added a note.

175
00:06:53,625 --> 00:06:56,090
They had received a great number of

176
00:06:56,110 --> 00:06:58,575
comments, almost all of them outraged by

177
00:06:58,595 --> 00:07:01,060
the way the corporal had been treated.

178
00:07:01,080 --> 00:07:03,900
The incident had been dramatized on the radio.

179
00:07:03,920 --> 00:07:06,740
That June, the New Yorker printed a short

180
00:07:06,760 --> 00:07:09,580
story based on it, called "A Short Wait

181
00:07:09,600 --> 00:07:10,400
between Trains."

182
00:07:11,550 --> 00:07:14,113
If you want more prisoner of war stories

183
00:07:14,133 --> 00:07:16,830
told from the documents, subscribe to WWII

184
00:07:16,850 --> 00:07:17,650
Frontlines.

185
00:07:19,100 --> 00:07:21,727
Trimmingham's lunchroom was not the only one.

186
00:07:21,747 --> 00:07:25,602
In the fall of 1942, an all-Black outfit

187
00:07:25,622 --> 00:07:28,222
came to Salina, Kansas, to open the new

188
00:07:28,242 --> 00:07:29,860
Smoky Hill Army Air Field.

189
00:07:29,880 --> 00:07:32,153
One of its men was Lloyd Brown,

190
00:07:32,172 --> 00:07:35,120
who rose to sergeant and later became a writer.

191
00:07:36,500 --> 00:07:39,231
German prisoners of war were in Salina too.

192
00:07:39,251 --> 00:07:41,982
One day at noon, Brown and several other

193
00:07:42,002 --> 00:07:44,389
men from his outfit walked into a

194
00:07:44,409 --> 00:07:45,421
lunchroom in town.

195
00:07:45,441 --> 00:07:48,172
They had come to see something with their

196
00:07:48,192 --> 00:07:48,992
own eyes.

197
00:07:50,150 --> 00:07:52,000
Brown told the story years later,

198
00:07:52,020 --> 00:07:53,558
in the New York Times.

199
00:07:53,578 --> 00:07:55,428
As they came in, he wrote,

200
00:07:55,448 --> 00:07:57,921
the counterman hurried to the back to get

201
00:07:57,941 --> 00:07:58,544
the owner.

202
00:07:58,564 --> 00:08:00,725
The owner hurried out to tell them,

203
00:08:00,745 --> 00:08:01,037
politely:

204
00:08:01,057 --> 00:08:03,218
"You boys know we don't serve colored

205
00:08:03,238 --> 00:08:04,038
here."

206
00:08:04,775 --> 00:08:06,002
They did not leave.

207
00:08:06,022 --> 00:08:07,562
They stood inside the door,

208
00:08:07,582 --> 00:08:10,056
staring at what they had come to see:

209
00:08:10,076 --> 00:08:11,616
the German prisoners of war,

210
00:08:11,636 --> 00:08:13,195
having lunch at the counter.

211
00:08:14,500 --> 00:08:16,918
Brown wrote that the people of Salina

212
00:08:16,938 --> 00:08:19,355
"would serve these enemy soldiers and turn

213
00:08:19,375 --> 00:08:21,445
away black American G.I.'s." And he

214
00:08:21,465 --> 00:08:24,579
finished with a sentence that is hard to forget.

215
00:08:24,599 --> 00:08:27,016
"If we were untermenschen in Nazi Germany,

216
00:08:27,036 --> 00:08:28,758
they would break our bones.

217
00:08:28,778 --> 00:08:30,499
As 'colored' men in Salina,

218
00:08:30,519 --> 00:08:32,260
they only break our hearts."

219
00:08:33,925 --> 00:08:35,986
The historian Matthias Reiss later studied

220
00:08:36,006 --> 00:08:37,027
letters like these.

221
00:08:37,047 --> 00:08:39,455
He found the same complaint from many

222
00:08:39,475 --> 00:08:42,231
parts of the country, written by men who

223
00:08:42,251 --> 00:08:43,985
had never met each other.

224
00:08:45,200 --> 00:08:48,500
In February 1944, a Black soldier named

225
00:08:48,520 --> 00:08:51,020
Bert Babero wrote from a camp in Texas

226
00:08:51,040 --> 00:08:53,855
that one part of the latrine was set aside

227
00:08:53,875 --> 00:08:56,375
for Negro soldiers, and the other for the

228
00:08:56,395 --> 00:08:58,265
German prisoners and the white soldiers.

229
00:08:58,285 --> 00:09:00,785
At least two camps in Mississippi gave the

230
00:09:00,805 --> 00:09:02,380
prisoners white water fountains too.

231
00:09:03,675 --> 00:09:05,325
In South Carolina, Charles Dryden,

232
00:09:05,345 --> 00:09:06,995
one of the Tuskegee Airmen,

233
00:09:07,015 --> 00:09:08,999
wrote that German prisoners could walk

234
00:09:09,019 --> 00:09:11,671
into the white side of the post exchange

235
00:09:11,691 --> 00:09:13,695
cafeteria, and his men could not.

236
00:09:14,950 --> 00:09:16,788
The historian John Hope Franklin,

237
00:09:16,808 --> 00:09:19,761
then a young man, remembered a segregated train.

238
00:09:19,781 --> 00:09:21,990
German prisoners on board watched the

239
00:09:22,010 --> 00:09:24,219
Black passengers packed into their car,

240
00:09:24,239 --> 00:09:27,192
and to them, he said, this was the

241
00:09:27,212 --> 00:09:27,935
funniest thing.

242
00:09:27,955 --> 00:09:29,070
They were laughing.

243
00:09:30,450 --> 00:09:33,739
And in January 1945, a Black newspaper

244
00:09:33,759 --> 00:09:35,916
reported that the National Association for

245
00:09:35,936 --> 00:09:38,094
the Advancement of Colored People had

246
00:09:38,114 --> 00:09:40,635
protested to the War Department about the

247
00:09:40,655 --> 00:09:42,812
prisoner of war camp at Florence,

248
00:09:42,832 --> 00:09:43,175
Arizona.

249
00:09:43,195 --> 00:09:46,079
Black Army nurses at the camp hospital had

250
00:09:46,099 --> 00:09:48,982
been ordered to eat in a separate dining

251
00:09:49,002 --> 00:09:51,160
room, while German prisoners served as

252
00:09:51,180 --> 00:09:53,701
cooks and waiters in the officers' mess.

253
00:09:53,721 --> 00:09:55,515
The prisoners, the complaint said,

254
00:09:55,535 --> 00:09:57,350
looked on with great glee.

255
00:09:59,075 --> 00:10:03,198
By 1944, Lena Horne was one of the most

256
00:10:03,218 --> 00:10:04,503
famous singers in America.

257
00:10:04,523 --> 00:10:06,787
She toured Army camps for the USO,

258
00:10:06,807 --> 00:10:09,396
and her contract said she would not sing

259
00:10:09,416 --> 00:10:10,395
for segregated audiences.

260
00:10:11,850 --> 00:10:14,395
That year, she came to Camp Robinson,

261
00:10:14,415 --> 00:10:15,860
outside Little Rock, Arkansas.

262
00:10:15,880 --> 00:10:18,425
Camp Robinson held German prisoners of war.

263
00:10:18,445 --> 00:10:21,355
The night before, she had sung for the

264
00:10:21,375 --> 00:10:23,920
white soldiers and workers on the post.

265
00:10:23,940 --> 00:10:27,217
The next night was meant to be for the

266
00:10:27,237 --> 00:10:28,037
Black soldiers.

267
00:10:29,325 --> 00:10:32,640
She came out on stage and looked at the audience.

268
00:10:32,660 --> 00:10:35,975
The Black soldiers were at the back of the hall.

269
00:10:35,995 --> 00:10:39,310
In the rows in front of them sat men she

270
00:10:39,330 --> 00:10:40,644
took for white soldiers.

271
00:10:40,664 --> 00:10:42,665
They were German prisoners of war.

272
00:10:43,925 --> 00:10:45,302
More than fifty years later,

273
00:10:45,322 --> 00:10:46,699
she told the story herself.

274
00:10:46,719 --> 00:10:48,933
"I went to the back where they were

275
00:10:48,953 --> 00:10:51,168
sitting," she said, "and sang with my back

276
00:10:51,188 --> 00:10:52,585
to all the other people."

277
00:10:53,900 --> 00:10:56,201
Her daughter, the writer Gail Buckley,

278
00:10:56,221 --> 00:10:57,362
told the rest.

279
00:10:57,382 --> 00:11:00,069
Her mother demanded to see the NAACP.

280
00:11:00,089 --> 00:11:02,391
And the NAACP arrived, Buckley said,

281
00:11:02,411 --> 00:11:04,712
in the person of one woman:

282
00:11:04,732 --> 00:11:07,806
Daisy Bates, who later became a hero of

283
00:11:07,826 --> 00:11:08,626
Little Rock.

284
00:11:09,925 --> 00:11:11,485
Then her tour was over.

285
00:11:11,505 --> 00:11:14,013
In Horne's words, they threw her out of

286
00:11:14,033 --> 00:11:14,645
the USO.

287
00:11:14,665 --> 00:11:17,805
So she paid her own way, and kept going to

288
00:11:17,825 --> 00:11:20,017
the camps to sing for Black soldiers

289
00:11:20,037 --> 00:11:22,565
whenever she could get space on a train.

290
00:11:23,900 --> 00:11:27,352
In January 1945, a Black newspaper in

291
00:11:27,372 --> 00:11:29,350
Omaha, Nebraska, reported that Lena Horne

292
00:11:29,370 --> 00:11:32,014
had called a halt to her Army tour.

293
00:11:32,034 --> 00:11:34,345
It told the story a little differently:

294
00:11:34,365 --> 00:11:36,675
that the commander at Camp Robinson had

295
00:11:36,695 --> 00:11:39,006
kept the camp's fifty Black soldiers out

296
00:11:39,026 --> 00:11:41,337
of her shows, while German prisoners of

297
00:11:41,357 --> 00:11:42,669
war were let in.

298
00:11:42,689 --> 00:11:45,020
Either way, it was the same insult.

299
00:11:46,725 --> 00:11:48,659
Some things were already changing.

300
00:11:48,679 --> 00:11:51,395
At first, Black soldiers had not been

301
00:11:51,415 --> 00:11:54,131
allowed to serve as escort guards for

302
00:11:54,151 --> 00:11:55,303
prisoners at all.

303
00:11:55,323 --> 00:11:59,115
In April 1944, the Army disbanded its

304
00:11:59,135 --> 00:12:00,665
segregated escort guard system.

305
00:12:01,950 --> 00:12:04,739
The story of the lunchroom went on without

306
00:12:04,759 --> 00:12:05,090
Trimmingham.

307
00:12:05,110 --> 00:12:06,846
After the New Yorker story,

308
00:12:06,866 --> 00:12:09,654
a Chicago theatre group made it into a

309
00:12:09,674 --> 00:12:13,630
play, first staged in October 1945.

310
00:12:13,650 --> 00:12:15,950
It came back on stage in 1957,

311
00:12:15,970 --> 00:12:19,975
and as a film on television in 1999.

312
00:12:20,675 --> 00:12:23,047
The last German prisoners went home from

313
00:12:23,067 --> 00:12:26,475
the United States by July 1946.

314
00:12:27,175 --> 00:12:29,412
Two years later, on the twenty-sixth of

315
00:12:29,432 --> 00:12:32,736
July, 1948, President Truman signed

316
00:12:32,756 --> 00:12:34,978
Executive Order nine nine eight one.

317
00:12:34,998 --> 00:12:37,594
It said "there shall be equality of

318
00:12:37,614 --> 00:12:39,837
treatment and opportunity for all persons

319
00:12:39,857 --> 00:12:42,453
in the armed services without regard to

320
00:12:42,473 --> 00:12:44,715
race, color, religion or national origin."

321
00:12:46,100 --> 00:12:48,410
It took years more to carry out.

322
00:12:48,430 --> 00:12:50,740
But the order was signed, and the

323
00:12:50,760 --> 00:12:52,404
segregated Army that Rupert Trimmingham

324
00:12:52,424 --> 00:12:55,420
had served in began to come to an end.

325
00:12:57,075 --> 00:13:01,175
Trimmingham was discharged in 1946.

326
00:13:01,195 --> 00:13:03,025
He moved to Gary, Indiana,

327
00:13:03,045 --> 00:13:05,615
and worked as an electrician for the

328
00:13:05,635 --> 00:13:07,095
Singer Sewing Machine Company.

329
00:13:07,115 --> 00:13:11,255
He became an American citizen in 1950.

330
00:13:11,275 --> 00:13:13,978
In 1956 he moved to Ann Arbor,

331
00:13:13,998 --> 00:13:14,798
Michigan.

332
00:13:15,625 --> 00:13:17,205
He died there on the ninth of May,

333
00:13:17,225 --> 00:13:20,565
1985, and was buried in Fairview Cemetery.

334
00:13:20,585 --> 00:13:23,272
His wife Harriet, whom he married in

335
00:13:23,292 --> 00:13:26,385
Arizona during the war, was buried beside him.

336
00:13:27,625 --> 00:13:30,245
Lloyd Brown went on to work with Paul

337
00:13:30,265 --> 00:13:32,555
Robeson and wrote books of his own.

338
00:13:32,575 --> 00:13:34,885
He died in New York in 2003.

339
00:13:36,300 --> 00:13:38,608
Lena Horne kept singing for decades.

340
00:13:38,628 --> 00:13:41,324
She died on the ninth of May,

341
00:13:41,344 --> 00:13:44,040
2010, twenty-five years to the day after

342
00:13:44,060 --> 00:13:46,000
the corporal from Fort Huachuca.

343
00:13:47,100 --> 00:13:49,049
If your father, grandfather or uncle

344
00:13:49,069 --> 00:13:51,017
served at Fort Huachuca, Camp Claiborne,

345
00:13:51,037 --> 00:13:52,657
Smoky Hill or Camp Robinson,

346
00:13:52,677 --> 00:13:54,298
or with the ninety-third Division,

347
00:13:54,318 --> 00:13:56,594
tell us his name in the comments.

348
00:13:56,614 --> 00:13:59,547
And if he ever told you about a lunchroom,

349
00:13:59,567 --> 00:14:02,520
a train or a show, tell us that too.

350
00:14:04,200 --> 00:14:06,700
Nobody knows the name of that town,

351
00:14:06,720 --> 00:14:08,140
or of that station.

352
00:14:08,160 --> 00:14:09,960
The lunchroom has no plaque.

353
00:14:11,250 --> 00:14:14,423
What is left is a letter, written by a

354
00:14:14,443 --> 00:14:16,907
corporal who bet that the Army's magazine

355
00:14:16,927 --> 00:14:17,971
would print it.

356
00:14:17,991 --> 00:14:19,035
He was right.

357
00:14:19,055 --> 00:14:21,519
And the question he asked that morning,

358
00:14:21,539 --> 00:14:23,647
standing outside, is still reprinted in

359
00:14:23,667 --> 00:14:24,357
history books.

360
00:14:24,377 --> 00:14:27,570
Then why are they treated better than we are?

